- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]  
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]  
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography]  
- Frequency of reading books is in [On everyday basis]  
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is Increase Academic Performance  
- How to manage books for reading is in [Used-all, Buy, Borrow]  
- Age is within range [21, 25]

---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Generally, gender does not strongly determine if a student reads academic books most of the time; no strong causal link.
- Age: Older undergraduates might have more academic responsibilities, possibly reading more academic books, but not a strict rule.
- Department: Students in academic or technical departments (like CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil) are more likely to read academic books most of the time compared to departments like English or BBA, where reading might be more varied.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading correlates positively with reading academic books if the reason is academic.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," the student likely reads academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Methods like "E-books," "Borrow," or "Used-all" might indicate active engagement with academic resources.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers suggest more academic reading if aligned with academic purpose.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, higher numbers suggest more academic reading.
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time: If favorite leisure books are "Non-Fiction" or "Biography," it may suggest an academic inclination, but if "Fiction" or "Politics," it might indicate leisure reading rather than academic.
- What is the change felt after reading books: Changes like "Increase Academic Performance" or "Increase Creative Thinking" suggest academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: Influence by "Teachers" or "Parents" may motivate academic reading.
- Which language books do you like to read: Preference for English might correlate with academic reading if the curriculum is in English.
- Where do you read printed books: Reading at "Home" or "Library" might indicate academic reading habits.
- Do you read Newspaper: Reading newspapers may indicate general reading habits but not necessarily academic reading.
- The format used for reading books: Using "Both" (printed and e-books) might indicate serious reading habits.
- Frequency of reading books: "On everyday basis" strongly correlates with reading academic books most of the time.

---

Step 2. Based on the above and example, infer 10 different conditions per answer class.

10 different conditions for class "0" (Does NOT read academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is in [Entertainment Purpose, Keep me informative, To increase my English vocabulary knowledge]
- Department is in [BBA, English, JMC, Others]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day < 1
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [1-5, nan]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [1-5, nan]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Fiction, Politics, Others]
- Frequency of reading books is in [Once a week, Occasionally, Rarely]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is in [Relax, No Change, Increase Interest in Entertainment]
- How to manage books for reading is in [Buy, Others]
- Do you read Newspaper is No

10 different conditions for class "1" (Reads academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is Academic Purpose
- Department is in [CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, MCT, CIS]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day >= 1
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography]
- Frequency of reading books is On everyday basis
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is Increase Academic Performance or Increase Creative Thinking
- How to manage books for reading is in [E-books, Borrow, Used-all]
- The format that you used for reading books is Both

